---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import GodFileChart from '../components/GodFileChart.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';

/**
 * Four data points is a stat display, not a chart. Only the god-file magnitudes
 * earn a plot; everything below is a table or a pair of numbers, deliberately.
 */
const authorship = [
  { file: 'Sentry · api/urls.py', engineers: 158, commits: 1086, since: '2013' },
  { file: 'Jenkins · SlaveComputer.java', engineers: 47, commits: 249, since: '2008' },
  { file: 'Excalidraw · App.tsx', engineers: 28, commits: 225, since: '—' },
  { file: 'Consul · “temporary” client', engineers: 8, commits: 14, since: '—' },
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Eight codebases, one shape of decay"
  metaTitle="The drift series — evidence-led product marketing | Tyler Young"
  description="Running a code-analysis tool against eight open-source codebases across seven languages, and building the go-to-market argument from what it found."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="Positioning + Narrative"
  meta={[
    ['Product', 'Invariant — a drift monitor for codebases'],
    ['Method', '8 repos · 7 languages · 5–22 years old'],
    ['Output', 'Series brief, 8-post arc, pitch, outreach strategy'],
    ['Contribution', 'Proposed the go-to-market motion; wrote the series, the pitch and the outreach strategy'],
    ['Not mine', 'The tool itself — built internally by the Alucify team'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      An internal tool sat inside the company doing useful work for the team that
      built it. I argued it was a product, and that the way to prove it was not to
      describe it — it was to run it. Point it at eight well-known open-source
      codebases, publish what it finds by name, and let the findings be the pitch.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>The premise</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Every codebase has unwritten rules — conventions 95% of files follow, with
      one quiet exception. None of them are bugs, and none appear in any
      architecture document. The tool learns those rules by reading the AST, then
      names where they broke. Its own framing of the stakes:
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <blockquote class="pull">
    Agents produce locally reasonable code — exactly what creates drift in the gap
    between “looks fine in this file” and “silently violates a convention three
    directories away.”
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Every codebase had one</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The most legible finding, and the one that opens the series: a single file
      that grew past the point anyone would have designed.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <GodFileChart />

  <h2>Nobody wrote them on purpose</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The authorship data is what turns the finding into an argument. Not one of
      these files has a single author. Each is the cumulative product of dozens of
      individually reasonable pull requests by people who never intended to build
      a god-file — which is precisely why no reviewer caught it.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption class="cap">Authorship spread on four of the god-files</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">File</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Engineers</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Commits</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Since</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          authorship.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{a.file}</td>
              <td class="num strong">{a.engineers}</td>
              <td class="num">{a.commits.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
              <td class="num dim">{a.since}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    <Draft>
      Consul's is the one to read twice. Eight engineers, fourteen commits — all
      under a commit message that literally says “remove resource api client from
      api module.” The file is still called temporary.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>The counter-example does the most work</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      A tool that finds problems everywhere is a tool nobody trusts. The series
      spends two of its eight posts on codebases where almost nothing was wrong,
      and that is the strategic move — it converts the instrument from an alarm
      into a measurement.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="stats">
    <div>
      <p class="n">66%</p>
      <p class="l">of Bitcoin Core's flagged candidates were real — the cleanest signal in the series</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="n">1</p>
      <p class="l">real drift across liquid-dsp's 947 files: one forgotten cleanup call, in the single test of 49 that omitted it</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="n">0</p>
      <p class="l">breaks in Bitcoin's consensus, script and crypto directories — every finding landed where the team doesn't watch</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    <Draft>
      That last number is the sharpest line in the whole series. Discipline did not
      prevent drift; it relocated it. The most adversarially reviewed codebase in
      open source was clean exactly where it was watched, and drifted everywhere
      else.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>The distribution plan is the product decision</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The outreach strategy refuses the obvious move. Not blog posts, not bulk
      reports — run the tool on a repository, find one real issue, write the fix
      yourself, and submit it as a pull request. The merged PR becomes a dated,
      attributed, public receipt that the tool found something real.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <blockquote class="pull">
    Issues alone read as drive-by criticism. Bulk tool reports read as spam. PRs
    with working fixes read as gifts.
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    <Draft>
      It is also disciplined about restraint: no bulk outreach, no templated PRs,
      no big corporate-backed projects, and nothing said publicly until three
      repositories have merged. Let the receipts accumulate before making a claim.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What was mine, and what wasn't</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The tool was not mine. Invariant was built inside the company for internal
      use, and I did not design or write it.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      What I did was see a product where the team saw a utility. I proposed running
      it against public codebases as a route to market, chose the eight repositories
      and the order they'd be published in, ran the analyses, wrote the series
      brief through ten drafts, the pitch through eight, and the outreach strategy
      — including the argument for why a merged pull request beats any blog post.
      The copy is in “we” because it was written for the company to send.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      That is a narrower claim than “I built this,” and a more useful one. The
      hardest part of a developer tool is rarely the tool.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Every figure on this page is quoted from the series brief, which cites the
      tool's own output. Nothing has been recomputed or estimated here. Written in
      collaboration with AI, like the rest of the material on this site.
    </p>
  </div>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-translated-design-work',
        note: 'Turning a technical finding into an argument a buyer can weigh is the same skill as mapping design work to business levers — the audience is just engineers rather than executives.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-built-a-shared-scoring-model',
        note: 'Publishing the codebases where the tool found almost nothing is a scoring decision: it makes the instrument legible by showing its floor, not just its ceiling.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }

  .pull {
    margin: 0 0 26px !important;
    padding: 18px 22px;
    background: var(--color-paper-sunk);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-signal);
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .table-wrap {
    margin: 0 0 26px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: var(--color-paper-raised);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 480px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  .cap {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  th {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  td {
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }

  tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .num {
    text-align: right;
  }

  .strong {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .dim {
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .stats {
    display: grid;
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 0 0 30px;
    padding: 24px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .stats .n {
    font-family: var(--font-display) !important;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 46px) !important;
    font-weight: 620;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1 !important;
    margin: 0 0 8px !important;
    color: var(--color-signal);
  }

  .stats .l {
    font-family: var(--font-mono) !important;
    font-size: 11px !important;
    line-height: 1.7 !important;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    margin: 0 !important;
  }

  @media (min-width: 810px) {
    .stats {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 32px;
    }
  }
</style>
